{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_584702223#3_1380512636","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly explains the post-2010 JGB/yield decline: since 2013 the Bank of Japan has bought large amounts of Japanese government bonds to keep long-term rates around 0%, and heavy buying raises bond prices while lowering yields; it also notes reduced market liquidity from aggressive BOJ policy.","learned":"BOJ bond-buying/quantitative easing since 2013 increased demand for JGBs, pushed prices up, kept 10-year yields near zero, and reduced JGB market liquidity."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2986","reason":"This query produced a highly relevant explanatory result, so continue down the ranked list for more documents tying falling JGB yields/market conditions to BOJ QE and deflation."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2986"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_63607313#4_133439606","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2986","rank":2,"snippet":"their yields, buying bonds and pushing up their price leads to lower longer-term rates. Under YCC, the central bank commits to buy whatever amount of bonds the market wants to supply at its target price. Once bond markets internalize the central bank’s commitment, the target price becomes the market price—who would be willing to sell the bond to a private investor for less than they could get by selling to the Fed? The Bank of Japan (BOJ) committed in 2016 to peg yields on 10-year <cover>Japanese Government Bonds (JGBs) around zero percent, in a fight to boost persistently low inflation. To hit that yield target, the BOJ </cover>has a standing offer to purchase any outstanding bond at a price consistent with the target yield. On days when private investors for any reason are less willing to pay that price, the BOJ ends up purchasing more bonds in order to keep yields inside the target price range. The Fed had some experience with interest rate pegs during and after World War II, when the Treasury needed help financing wartime expenditures. In 1942, the Fed and Treasury internally agreed that the Fed would cap the Treasury’s borrowing "}}
