{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_63607313#4_133439606","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly explains why JGB yields were held down: under yield-curve control, the BOJ committed in 2016 to peg 10-year Japanese Government Bond yields around zero to fight persistently low inflation, buying bonds as needed to maintain the target.","learned":"BOJ yield-curve control from 2016 pegged 10-year JGB yields near zero through standing bond purchases, motivated by persistently low inflation."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2986","reason":"The current query is producing grade-3 evidence about BOJ policy and low inflation as causes of falling/low JGB yields, so continue to collect more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2986"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_63607313#3_133437312","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2986","rank":3,"snippet":"the Fed pursued during the Great Recession and is pursuing in 2020. QE deals in quantities of bonds; YCC focuses on prices of bonds. Under QE, a central bank might announce that it plans to purchase, for instance, $1 trillion in Treasury securities. Because bond prices are inversely related to their yields, buying bonds and pushing up their price leads to lower longer-term rates. Under YCC, the central bank commits to buy whatever amount of bonds the market wants to supply at its target price. Once bond markets internalize the central bank’s commitment, the target price becomes the market price—who would be willing to sell the bond to a private investor for less than they could get by selling to the Fed? The Bank of Japan (<cover>BOJ) committed in 2016 to peg yields on 10-year Japanese Government Bonds (JGBs) around zero percent, in a fight to boost persistently low inflation.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 2089.000000 , \"title\": \"What is yield curve control?\" , \"url\": \"https://www.brookings.edu/blog/up-front/2020/06/05/what-is-yield-curve-control/\" }"}}
