{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ japan japanese) (+ \"government bond yields\" \"bond yields\" jgb) (+ \"quantitative easing\" \"asset purchases\" \"bond purchases\") (+ 2013 abenomics))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1396036188#7_1572983972","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2997","rank":1,"snippet":": 8277.000000 , \"headings\": \"Quantitative easing<cover>Quantitative easing Contents Process and benefits History Precedents Japan before 2007 After 2007 US QE1, QE2, and QE3 US QE4 United Kingdom Eurozone Switzerland Sweden Japan after 2007 and Abenomics Effectiveness of QE Risks and side-effects Impact on savings and pensions Effects on climate change Increased income and wealth inequality International spillovers for BRICs and emerging economies Moral hazard Reputational risks Alternative policies QE for the people Fiscal policy Monetary financing Neo-Fisherism Collateral and credit easing References External links \" , \"segment\": \"And when demand for financial assets is high, the value of these assets increases. This makes businesses and households holding shares wealthier – making them more likely to spend more, boosting economic activity. Signalling effect: Some economists argue that QE's main impact is due to its effect on the psychology of the markets, by signalling that the central bank will take extraordinary steps to facilitate economic recovery. For instance, it has been observed that most of the effect of QE in the Eurozone on bond yields </cover>happened between the date of the announcement of QE and the actual start "}}
