{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1396036188#7_1572983972","relevance":1,"reason":"The snippet is about quantitative easing in general and only mentions Japan/Abenomics in headings; it does not directly explain why Japanese bond yields or JGB market activity have dropped since 2010.","learned":"A broad QE/Abenomics query can retrieve generic quantitative-easing material rather than focused Japanese bond-market explanations."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1396036188#7_1572983972","reason":"Although the snippet is generic, the document headings include 'Japan after 2007 and Abenomics'; the full document may contain direct evidence about BOJ QE effects on Japanese bond yields after 2010."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1396036188#7_1572983972"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1396036188#7_1572983972","document":"Quantitative easing - Wikipedia ... Quantitative easing Quantitative easing Contents Process and benefits History Precedents Japan before 2007 After 2007 US QE1, QE2, and QE3 US QE4 United Kingdom Eurozone Switzerland Sweden Japan after 2007 and Abenomics Effectiveness of QE Risks and side-effects Impact on savings and pensions Effects on climate change Increased income and wealth inequality International spillovers for BRICs and emerging economies Moral hazard Reputational risks Alternative policies QE for the people Fiscal policy Monetary financing Neo-Fisherism Collateral and credit easing References External links  ... And when demand for financial assets is high, the value of these assets increases. This makes businesses and households holding shares wealthier – making them more likely to spend more, boosting economic activity. Signalling effect: Some economists argue that QE's main impact is due to its effect on the psychology of the markets, by signalling that the central bank will take extraordinary steps to facilitate economic recovery. For instance, it has been observed that most of the effect of QE in the Eurozone on bond yields happened between the date of the announcement of QE and the actual start of the purchases by the ECB. History Precedents The US Federal Reserve belatedly implemented policies similar to the recent quantitative easing during the Great Depression of the 1930s. Specifically, banks' excess reserves exceeded 6 percent in 1940, whereas they vanished during the entire postwar period until 2008. Despite this fact, many commentators called the scope of the Federal Reserve quantitative easing program after the 2008 crisis \\\"unprecedented\\\". Japan before 2007 A policy termed \\\"quantitative easing\\\" (量的金融緩和, ryōteki kin'yū kanwa) was first used by the Bank of Japan (BOJ) to fight domestic deflation in the early 2000s. The BOJ had maintained short-term interest rates at close to zero since 1999.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"13/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1396036188#7_1572983972","relevance":2,"reason":"The full document provides some supporting background: QE raises demand for financial assets and can lower bond yields, and the BOJ used QE to fight deflation while keeping rates near zero. However, it is a general QE article and is not dedicated to why the Japanese bond market/JGB yields have been dropping since 2010.","learned":"General mechanism: central-bank quantitative easing increases demand for financial assets, raising prices and lowering yields; the BOJ used QE against deflation and near-zero rates."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ japan negative \"government bond\" yields \"bank of japan\" deflation)","reason":"Search for focused explanations of Japan's negative/very low government bond yields that explicitly combine BOJ policy and deflation, likely to yield direct grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ japan negative \"government bond\" yields \"bank of japan\" deflation)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_325219708#2_747880036","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2998","rank":3,"snippet":"Rates Interest rates are a monetary policy tool used by central banks to influence inflation throughout an economy. A central bank attempts to combat <cover>deflation by reducing interest rates in order to encourage consumers and businesses to use more loans. This increases demand, which raises prices. This is one of the many conventional monetary policies. 3 Conventional monetary policies have not been as effective in recent times. A newer train of thought is for a country's central bank to lower interest rates past zero, into negative rates. This move is designed to incentivize banks to lend money while influencing businesses and consumers to spend, rather than pay fees in order to keep their cash in an account at a bank. The European Central Bank introduced its negative interest rate policy in 2014; in January of 2016, the Bank of Japan unexpectedly did the same, cutting its benchmark rates below zero in a bold move to stimulate its economy and overcome persistent deflationary pressures. 2 The graph below illustrates Japan's 10-year government bond yields </cover>from 2012 through the present.\" , \"start_char\": 1539.000000 , \"title\": \"What Negative ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1053083924#1_2120551809","rank":1,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_387700658#4_656328526","rank":2,"relevance":2}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"13/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_325219708#2_747880036","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage explains that central banks cut rates, even below zero, to fight deflation, and notes the Bank of Japan adopted negative rates in 2016 to stimulate the economy and overcome persistent deflationary pressures, with reference to Japan's 10-year government bond yields. It supports the answer but is a broader negative-rates article rather than a dedicated explanation of Japan's bond-market decline since 2010.","learned":"BOJ negative interest-rate policy in 2016 was aimed at stimulating the economy and overcoming persistent deflation, contributing to very low or negative JGB yields."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"jgb market\" \"japanese government bond market\") (+ liquidity trading) (+ decline declined \"dried up\" \"dried\") (+ \"bank of japan\" boj))","reason":"Search directly for documents explaining declining liquidity/trading in the Japanese government bond market due to Bank of Japan actions, matching the possible meaning of 'bond market dropping' as market activity rather than yields."}}
