{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_607479046#4_1430854876","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly explains a key reason Japanese government bond yields stayed down after 2010: in late 2016 the BOJ shifted from QE to yield-curve control, pegging 10-year JGB yields at 0% and buying bonds whenever market yields rose above target to stimulate the economy.","learned":"BOJ yield-curve control pegs 10-year JGB yields around 0%; when yields rise, the BOJ buys bonds to push them back down."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3009","reason":"This result list contains many relevant YCC/JGB/BOJ passages and may yield additional distinct grade-3 explanations of why Japanese bond yields have been held down."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3009"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_607479046#3_1430852992","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3009","rank":8,"snippet":"specific long-term interest rate target and buy as many bonds as necessary to achieve it. YCC would set a specific price for the bonds in terms of their yield. 2 ﻿ Examples of YCC Programs During World War II, massive borrowing by the U.S. federal government was necessary to fund the war effort. However, this threatened to send interest rates soaring, making such debt increasingly more burdensome to service. In response, from 1942 to roughly 1947, the Fed successfully kept the government's borrowing costs down by purchasing any government bond that yielded more than certain targeted rates. Notably, the government was able to reach its goals with relatively modest bond purchases. 9 ﻿ 2 ﻿ More recently, the <cover>Bank of Japan (BoJ) shifted in late 2016 from a policy of QE to one of YCC, in which it sought to peg the yield on 10-year Japanese Government Bonds (</cover>JGBs) at 0%, in a effort to stimulate Japan's economy.\" , \"start_char\": 1884.000000 , \"title\": \"Yield Curve Control Definition\" , \"url\": \"https://www.investopedia.com/what-is-yield-curve-control-4797189\" }"}}
