{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_607479046#3_1430852992","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a near-duplicate passage from the same Investopedia yield-curve-control article as the previous result, but it directly explains that the BOJ shifted from QE to YCC in late 2016 to peg 10-year JGB yields at 0% in order to stimulate Japan's economy.","learned":"Repeated evidence: BOJ yield-curve control since late 2016 targeted 10-year JGB yields at 0%, suppressing yields through bond purchases."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ japan japanese) (+ \"bond yields\" \"government bond yields\" jgb) (+ low \"so low\" negative) (+ debt deflation \"bank of japan\" investors))","reason":"Search for broader Japan-focused explanations of why JGB yields are low despite high debt, likely covering BOJ policy, deflation, and domestic investor demand as direct causes of falling/low Japanese bond yields since 2010."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ japan japanese) (+ \"bond yields\" \"government bond yields\" jgb) (+ low \"so low\" negative) (+ debt deflation \"bank of japan\" investors))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_589063116#8_1390242598","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3010","rank":1,"snippet":"loss merely from the currency exchange fluctuation, irrespective of the yield and price of the bond investment. Domestically, investors might expect a period of deflation, or lower prices in the economy, which would allow them to make money by using their savings to buy more goods and services. Safe Haven Assets Investors might also be interested in negative bond yields if the loss is less than it would be with another investment. In times of economic uncertainty, many investors rush to buy bonds because they're considered safe-haven investments. These purchases are called the flight-to-safety-trade in the bond market. During such a time, investors might accept a negative-yielding bond because the negative yield might be far less of a loss than a potential double-digit percentage loss in the equity markets. For example, <cover>Japanese Government Bonds (JGB) are popular safe-haven assets for international investors and have, at times, paid a negative </cover>yield.\" , \"start_char\": 5182.000000 , \"title\": \"What Is a Negative Bond Yield?\" , \"url\": \"https://www.investopedia.com/terms/n/negative-bond-yield.asp\" }"}}
