{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_589063116#9_1390244599","relevance":2,"reason":"This is a near-duplicate from the same Investopedia negative-bond-yield article as the previous result. It supports the answer by noting JGBs can have negative yields because investors treat them as safe-haven assets and may accept losses in uncertain markets, but it is not dedicated to explaining Japan's post-2010 bond-market/yield decline.","learned":"Repeated evidence: safe-haven demand helps explain why investors may buy JGBs even when yields are negative."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ japan japanese) (+ \"10-year bond yield\" \"10-year government bond yield\" \"10-year jgb yield\") (+ low negative \"near zero\") (+ deflation \"monetary easing\" \"bank of japan\"))","reason":"Search for benchmark 10-year Japanese bond yield explanations using direct yield language and causal factors such as deflation, BOJ monetary easing, and near-zero/negative rates."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ japan japanese) (+ \"10-year bond yield\" \"10-year government bond yield\" \"10-year jgb yield\") (+ low negative \"near zero\") (+ deflation \"monetary easing\" \"bank of japan\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_1576090271#11_3229555823","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3011","rank":3,"snippet":"In 2018 Conclusion \" , \"segment\": \"If prices stayed the same or went down and inflation was instead 0% or less, which would be negative interest rates, then we'd all be less likely to keep our money in the bank. We'd all be storing it in safes and or buried somewhere in our backyards. See What Happened In Japan This is what happened in Japan when rates went negative in 2016: This is the <cover>10-year government bond yield. It was negative .23% for 10 years in June 2016. Fortune wrote an article in February 2016 describing how the Japanese were turning to hoarding their yen in safes. \"Following the Bank of Japan’</cover>s decision to lower interest rates below zero in January, many consumers have reportedly rushed to hardware stores in search of one thing: Safes. Negative interest rates mean customers effectively pay a fee for parking cash in banks, so Japanese citizens are beginning to hoard yen, according to the Wall Street Journal, and they need somewhere to put it.\" Notice that rates didn't stay negative for too long.\" , \"start_char\": 5291.000000 ,","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_33_1289489410#9_2747135624","rank":1,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_33_1289489410#8_2747133663","rank":2,"relevance":2}]}}
