{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_10_106621170#1_202791124","relevance":1,"reason":"The passage is about Japan's policy interest rate reaching a record low of -0.10% in 2016 and provides general economic data. It is related to the low-rate environment behind low JGB yields, but it does not directly explain why the Japanese bond market or JGB yields have been dropping since 2010.","learned":"Japan's policy interest rate reached a record low of -0.10% in January 2016, reinforcing the low-rate environment but not directly explaining the JGB market decline."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ japan japanese jgb) (+ \"bond market\" \"government bond market\") (+ \"dried up\" \"dry up\" liquidity trading) (+ \"bank of japan\" boj))","reason":"Search for direct evidence that Japanese/JGB bond-market liquidity or trading has dried up due to Bank of Japan actions, matching the user's possible meaning that the bond market itself has been dropping rather than yields."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ japan japanese jgb) (+ \"bond market\" \"government bond market\") (+ \"dried up\" \"dry up\" liquidity trading) (+ \"bank of japan\" boj))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_873147407#3_1843493522","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3014","rank":1,"snippet":"2. Collateral management 3. Rebuilding confidence 4. Regulation About the author \" , \"segment\": \"Main reasons for the increase in turnover One reason behind this increase in turnover was the intensive trading of yen pairs following the <cover>Bank of Japan's monetary policy regime shift in early April 2013, which triggered a phase of exceptionally high turnover across asset classes. In the months that followed, particularly during the summer, the rise in yen trading partly reversed. Even without this yen effect, however, FX turnover would probably still have grown by about 25%. Another reason for the higher turnover is the growth of investment in international assets. With yields in advanced economies at record lows, investors increasingly diversified into riskier assets such as international equities and local currency emerging market bonds. Over the past three years, equities have provided investors with attractive returns, emerging market bond spreads have simultaneously dropped, and issuance in riskier bond market </cover>segments (eg local currency emerging market bonds) has soared. Not only did these three factors give rise to the need to trade FX in large quantities and to rebalance portfolios more frequently, but it also went hand in hand with greater "}}
