{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1163755555#9_2473954257","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage gives direct supporting evidence for low Japanese government-bond yields: prolonged deflation makes low-yielding government debt more attractive in real-return terms, and domestic Japanese investors have been satisfied with those returns. It also notes aging/shrinking population and savings constraints, but the document is about Japan's debt crisis rather than dedicated to explaining the post-2010 bond-market decline.","learned":"Persistent deflation can make low nominal JGB yields attractive because real returns remain positive; domestic investor demand/savings helped absorb Japanese government debt, though aging may threaten that support."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3016","reason":"This query is producing macroeconomic explanations for low Japanese bond yields beyond BOJ policy, so continue for more direct evidence on deflation, demographics, savings, and investor demand."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3016"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1163755555#8_2473952252","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3016","rank":3,"snippet":"tax income. Note that we also assume interest rate to stay at 1.1%, because if this figure increases then the government will default more quickly. An Economic Pandemic As we stated above, Japan is still on the high ground because the government can sell most of its debts to its citizens, which is known as domestically held debt. Deflation (decreasing prices of goods and services) that occurs for long periods of time make government debt and other <cover>low yielding assets much more attractive. For example, if the price of goods drops by 1% while government bond yields 1%, then the overall return would be 2%. Japanese investors are currently satisfied with these returns, but the country has to increase national savings </cover>so that domestic purchasers continue to buy new government debt. Yet Japan’s population is shrinking and aging, so it’s highly doubtful that the country can increase national savings to a point where purchasing government bonds is sustainable. And because Japan is a net importer of goods, the only way to reduce debt is by having foreign investors.\" , \"start_char\": 4878.000000 , \"title\": \"When Will ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_387700658#4_656328526","rank":2,"relevance":2}]}}
